  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: Dyslexia: Reading Differently.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "The letters seem to dance on the page. You read the same line three times and it still doesn't stick.",
      "visual": "📖"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Dyslexia is a learning difference that specifically affects reading and language processing despite normal intelligence.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Brain imaging reveals underactivation in the left temporoparietal region during phonological processing.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Dyslexic brains often show stronger right-hemisphere activity—linked to enhanced spatial reasoning and creativity.",
      "visual": "🎨"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "With early, structured phonics instruction, dyslexic brains can develop alternative reading pathways.",
      "visual": "🛤️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Dyslexia isn't about intelligence. It's about a brain that reads through a different door.",
      "visual": "🚪"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What brain region is underactive during reading in dyslexia?",
    "options": [
      "The left temporoparietal region",
      "The right occipital lobe",
      "The cerebellum"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
